EXT["contents-cleaning"] = """
<h2>Restore or replace is an arithmetic question</h2>
<p>For every affected item there is a cost to clean it and a cost to replace it, and the right answer is whichever is lower &mdash; except where the item is irreplaceable, in which case restoration is attempted regardless of the arithmetic.</p>
<p>Cleaning generally wins on furniture of real quality, on electronics, on anything custom or built to order with a long lead time, and on the whole category of things that have no replacement value but enormous personal value. Replacement generally wins on mass-market soft goods, on items that were near the end of their life anyway, and on anything porous that was exposed to Category 3 water.</p>
<p>We give you the comparison rather than making the decision for you, because the sentimental weight of an object is not something we can assess from the outside.</p>

<h2>The cleaning methods and what each is for</h2>
<h3>Ultrasonic</h3>
<p>Items are immersed in a tank where high-frequency sound generates microscopic cavitation bubbles that collapse against the surface, lifting contamination out of detail and crevices that no cloth reaches. It is the right method for hard, non-porous goods &mdash; kitchenware, glassware, metal fixtures, tools, blinds, toys, ornaments &mdash; and it is dramatically more effective on smoke residue than hand cleaning.</p>
<h3>Hydrocarbon and specialist textile washing</h3>
<p>Soft contents affected by smoke or contaminated water are processed in systems that use hydraulic action and specialist detergents to flush contamination out of fabric rather than redistributing it. Clothing, bedding, soft toys, curtains and many upholstered pieces that would previously have been written off can be recovered this way, including from Category 3 exposure in many cases.</p>
<h3>Hand cleaning</h3>
<p>Furniture, wood, leather and anything too large or too delicate for immersion. Slower, and unavoidable.</p>
<h3>Document and photograph recovery</h3>
<p>Wet paper is a race. Vacuum freeze-drying &mdash; freezing the material and then subliming the ice directly to vapour under vacuum &mdash; recovers documents, books and photographs that would be destroyed by ordinary air drying. Getting affected paper frozen quickly buys the time to do it properly, and it is the single most useful thing that can be done with wet documents in the first 48 hours.</p>

<h2>Electronics: do not turn it on</h2>
<p>This is the most valuable sentence on this page. If a device has been exposed to water, smoke or heat, powering it up is what destroys it.</p>
<p>Smoke residue is acidic and hygroscopic &mdash; it draws moisture out of the air and holds it against circuitry, and corrosion begins within days and continues indefinitely until the residue is removed. Applying current to a board with conductive residue on it creates shorts. A device that is dead-cold and contaminated is frequently recoverable; the same device after someone tried it is frequently not.</p>
<p>Leave it off, leave it unplugged, and let it be assessed and cleaned by someone with the right process.</p>

<h2>Inventory discipline</h2>
<p>Every item is inventoried with photographs and condition notes before processing, tracked through cleaning, and reconciled on return. Items assessed as non-salvageable are documented to the same standard before disposal, because that record is what your contents claim settles against.</p>
<p>Where an item cannot be fully restored we say so before it comes back, rather than returning it and letting you find the residual damage yourself.</p>
"""

# ------------------------------------------------------------- SMOKE
EXT["smoke-damage-cleanup"] = """
<h2>Why smoke keeps causing damage after the fire is out</h2>
<p>Fire damage stops when the fire stops. Smoke damage does not &mdash; it continues actively for as long as the residue is in the building, and the timeline is faster than most people expect.</p>
<p>Within hours, acidic residue begins etching glass, marble, chrome and porcelain. Within days, metals begin corroding and plastics begin to discolour permanently. Within weeks, the discolouration of walls, ceilings and grout stops being cleanable and becomes a refinishing job. Uncoated metal, electronics contacts and appliance components are the first casualties.</p>
<p>This is the entire reason smoke work is urgent even when the visible damage looks stable. Every week of delay converts cleanable surfaces into replaceable ones and grows the claim.</p>

<h2>The HVAC system is the distribution network</h2>
<p>If the system ran during or after the fire, smoke is in the ductwork, on the coil, in the blower housing and in the filter media &mdash; and every subsequent cycle redistributes it into rooms that were never touched by the fire. It is the reason a bedroom at the far end of a house smells of smoke when the fire was in the kitchen.</p>
<p>The system stays off until it has been addressed. Ducts are cleaned, the coil and blower are cleaned, filters are replaced, and where residue has penetrated flex duct interior the duct is replaced rather than cleaned, because flex duct interior does not clean reliably.</p>

<h2>Cleaning is matched to residue type</h2>
<p>Using the wrong method makes the damage permanent, which is why identifying the residue comes before touching anything.</p>
<p><strong>Dry smoke</strong> from fast, hot, high-oxygen fires leaves a powdery residue that must be dry-cleaned first &mdash; HEPA vacuuming and chemical sponge &mdash; because introducing moisture turns the powder into a smear that sets into the surface.</p>
<p><strong>Wet smoke</strong> from slow, smouldering, low-oxygen fires leaves a sticky, dense, strongly odorous residue that requires solvent-based cleaning and considerable mechanical effort.</p>
<p><strong>Protein residue</strong> from cooking fires is nearly invisible, coats everything as a thin varnish, and needs degreasing agents.</p>
<p><strong>Fuel-oil soot</strong> from furnace puff-backs is heavy, greasy and stains aggressively.</p>
<p>Most real fires produce more than one type in different parts of the property, so a single method applied throughout will fail somewhere.</p>

<h2>Why household cleaning makes it worse</h2>
<p>Wiping dry residue with a wet cloth drives it into the surface and sets it. General-purpose cleaners on acidic residue can react and etch. Vacuuming with a domestic vacuum lacking HEPA filtration blows fine particulate straight back into the air and into rooms that were clean.</p>
<p>The most common version of this we see is a homeowner who spent a weekend scrubbing walls that could have been cleaned, and now needs them sealed and repainted.</p>

<h2>Sealing before finish</h2>
<p>Where residue has penetrated framing, subfloor or substrate that stays in the building, those surfaces are sealed with a shellac-based product after cleaning and before any new finish goes on. Paint over unsealed smoke-affected substrate will let odour through within months and the whole surface has to be redone.</p>
"""

# ------------------------------------------------------------- STRUCTURAL DRYING
EXT["structural-drying"] = """
<h2>The physics, briefly</h2>
<p>Drying is moving water from inside a material into the air, and then out of the building. Both halves have to work. Air movement alone raises humidity until the air cannot accept more moisture and drying stalls. Dehumidification alone removes moisture from air that materials are not releasing into. Together, and balanced, they work.</p>
<p>The driving force is vapour pressure differential &mdash; the difference between moisture pressure inside the material and moisture pressure in the surrounding air. Larger differential, faster drying. Dehumidification lowers the air side; air movement breaks up the saturated boundary layer sitting against the material surface; heat raises the material side. Get all three right and a structure dries in days. Get the balance wrong and it does not dry at all, regardless of how much equipment is running.</p>

<h2>Grains per pound, and why we measure it</h2>
<p>Relative humidity is a poor working measure because it changes with temperature without any moisture entering or leaving. Grains per pound &mdash; the absolute mass of water vapour in a pound of dry air &mdash; does not. It is the number that tells us whether a dehumidifier is actually removing water.</p>
<p>We record GPP inside the drying chamber, in unaffected areas of the property, and outside. Chamber readings should be meaningfully drier than the reference readings. If they are not, either the equipment is undersized, the containment is leaking, or there is a moisture source still feeding the space.</p>

<h2>Refrigerant, LGR and desiccant</h2>
<p><strong>Conventional refrigerant</strong> dehumidifiers condense moisture on a cold coil. They are effective while the air is warm and reasonably humid and lose effectiveness as conditions dry out.</p>
<p><strong>Low-grain refrigerant (LGR)</strong> units pre-cool incoming air, allowing them to keep pulling moisture at much lower grain levels. They are the workhorse of residential structural drying and they are what makes a genuine dry standard achievable rather than approximate.</p>
<p><strong>Desiccant</strong> dehumidifiers adsorb moisture onto a chemical wheel rather than condensing it, so they keep working in cold conditions and can drive vapour pressure far lower than refrigerant units. They are the tool for large commercial volumes, cold-weather work, and difficult materials like hardwood and plaster that need a strong differential to release bound moisture.</p>

<h2>Specialty drying, and why it saves floors</h2>
<p>Standard air movers dry surfaces. Water trapped inside an assembly needs to be reached directly.</p>
<p><strong>Wall cavity injection</strong> forces dry air through drilled ports into the cavity, drying insulation and framing without removing drywall. On a wet wall that would otherwise be cut to two feet, this frequently saves the entire wall and the paint on it.</p>
<p><strong>Hardwood floor drying mats</strong> seal to the floor surface and pull moisture up through the boards under vacuum. Applied fast enough, they routinely save hardwood that would otherwise cup, crown and require full replacement &mdash; and hardwood replacement is one of the largest line items on a residential water claim.</p>
<p><strong>Sub-floor and crawlspace drying</strong> targets the assembly from beneath where access allows.</p>

<h2>The dry standard</h2>
<p>Drying is complete when affected materials reach the moisture content of the same materials in unaffected parts of the building, verified with a meter, not when the equipment has been running a certain number of days and not when surfaces feel dry.</p>
<p>Readings are taken daily at the same documented points, and the log goes to your carrier. When materials hit the standard, equipment comes out and rebuild can start. Closing a wall over a structure that has not hit the standard is how a water loss becomes a mold claim four months later.</p>
"""

