EXT["storm-damage-restoration"] = """
<h2>Hail damage is often invisible from the ground</h2>
<p>The damage that matters on a composition shingle roof is not a hole. It is bruising &mdash; impact that fractures the mat beneath the surface and dislodges the granules protecting the asphalt from ultraviolet light. The shingle looks intact from the driveway and it is no longer weatherproof. Granule loss accelerates, the asphalt degrades, and the roof begins leaking one to three years later, long after the storm date and long after the claim window closed.</p>
<p>The reliable indicators are soft metal accessories &mdash; gutters, downspouts, vents, flashing, air-conditioner condenser fins, mailboxes and window screens. Hail leaves visible dents in soft metal at the same impact energy that bruises shingles. If the condenser fins are dimpled, the roof took the same hits.</p>
<p>This is the reason we recommend an inspection after any significant hail event in your area even if nothing appears wrong. Establishing damage on a date is straightforward; establishing it three years later against a carrier's argument that it is wear is not.</p>

<h2>Wind, and the uplift sequence</h2>
<p>Wind damage progresses in a predictable order. Uplift breaks the adhesive seal between shingle courses, and a shingle that is sealed only at the nail line lifts in every subsequent wind. Water then drives under the course during rain, reaches the decking, and the failure is well established before anything shows on a ceiling.</p>
<p>Missing shingles are the obvious sign. Lifted-but-not-missing shingles are the common one, and they are visible from a ladder and invisible from the ground.</p>

<h2>The first 48 hours</h2>
<p>Cover the roof properly &mdash; a battened tarp, fastened above the damage line, extending well past the affected area. Photograph everything before any temporary work goes on, including the debris and the ground-level indicators. Note the date and time of the storm, which your carrier will ask for. Move contents out from under active leaks and get containers under drips. Keep every receipt for emergency measures.</p>
<p>Then get the claim opened. Carriers work through post-storm volume in the order it arrives, and after a major DFW event the queue is measured in weeks.</p>

<h2>Storm-chasing contractors</h2>
<p>Within 72 hours of any significant hail event, out-of-state crews work affected neighbourhoods door to door. Some are competent. Many are not, and a proportion are running a business model that depends on being gone before the workmanship fails.</p>
<p>The specific warnings: anyone pressuring you to sign on the doorstep; anyone offering to waive, absorb or cover your deductible, which is illegal in Texas and makes you party to insurance fraud; anyone asking for an assignment of benefits before you have read it, which transfers control of your claim to them; anyone who cannot give you a physical local address; and anyone requesting substantial payment before materials are delivered.</p>
<p>Ask any contractor &mdash; us included &mdash; for a local address, current proof of general liability insurance, and references from work in your own city. A company that will still be here in five years to honour a workmanship warranty can answer all three in a minute.</p>
"""

