EXT2["contents-cleaning"] = """
<h2>The items people ask about most</h2>
<p><strong>Wedding dresses and christening gowns.</strong> Frequently recoverable even from smoke and contaminated water, and worth attempting regardless of the arithmetic. Do not attempt to clean or dry it yourself first &mdash; the most common reason these are unrecoverable is a well-meant wash at home.</p>
<p><strong>Musical instruments.</strong> Pianos, string instruments and brass all respond badly to humidity swings and need controlled conditions immediately rather than eventually. Specialist restoration is usually a separate trade and we will bring in the right person rather than pretending otherwise.</p>
<p><strong>Firearms.</strong> Corrode fast after water or smoke exposure and need oiling and attention within days.</p>
<p><strong>Artwork and framed photographs.</strong> Remove from frames only if the surface is not adhering to the glass. If it is stuck, leave it and let a conservator separate it &mdash; pulling it apart destroys the image.</p>
<p><strong>Vinyl records, film and negatives.</strong> Recoverable if kept wet and cool rather than allowed to dry stuck together.</p>

<h2>The realistic limits</h2>
<p>Some things do not come back. Particleboard and MDF furniture delaminates once it is wet and cannot be restored. Mattresses and upholstered furniture exposed to Category 3 water are removed as a matter of protocol. Items with deep, set-in charring are structurally gone. Heavily corroded electronics past a certain point are not economically recoverable even when they are technically recoverable.</p>
<p>We would rather tell you an item is finished at the assessment than take it away, charge to attempt it, and return it in the same condition six weeks later.</p>
"""

EXT2["smoke-damage-cleanup"] = """
<h2>Where smoke goes that nobody looks</h2>
<p>Smoke follows air, and air goes everywhere in a building. The result is that smoke residue is found consistently in places well away from the fire, and a job scoped only to the visibly affected rooms will leave odour behind.</p>
<p>The reliable hiding places: inside wall cavities via outlet and switch boxes, which is why receptacles in unaffected rooms often smell; the attic, reached through ceiling penetrations and can lights; inside closets and drawers, where still air holds residue and where soft goods absorb it; on top of upper cabinets and behind appliances; inside the HVAC plenum and returns; and in carpet cushion, which acts as a filter for airborne particulate for as long as the air keeps moving through the house.</p>
<p>Part of the assessment is checking those locations specifically rather than scoping to what is visible, and it is the main reason a professional smoke scope looks larger than a homeowner expects.</p>

<h2>Timeline and what determines it</h2>
<p>A small contained fire with limited smoke spread is typically a matter of days. A whole-house smoke event with HVAC contamination and contents processing runs weeks, and the contents work usually runs longer than the structural work because ultrasonic and textile processing is a queue.</p>
<p>The variable that moves the timeline most is how quickly the work starts. Residue that has been in place a week cleans; residue that has been in place two months has bonded, etched and corroded, and the cleaning scope becomes a refinishing and replacement scope.</p>
"""

EXT2["odor-removal"] = """
<h2>Odour in the HVAC system</h2>
<p>If a property has an odour that returns whenever the air conditioning runs, the system is the reservoir. Residue, biological growth on the evaporator coil, contaminated filter media and material lodged in the ductwork all recirculate every cycle, and no amount of cleaning elsewhere in the building will fix it.</p>
<p>Addressing it means cleaning the ducts, the coil, the blower housing and the plenum, replacing filters, and replacing flex duct where residue has reached the interior lining &mdash; because flex duct interior does not clean to a standard you can rely on. On properties with a history of moisture in the system, an ultraviolet coil treatment is worth considering as a preventive measure afterward.</p>

<h2>Odour in new properties, and what it usually is</h2>
<p>Not every persistent smell is a restoration problem. Before committing to treatment we check for the ordinary explanations, because they are common and fixing them costs almost nothing.</p>
<p>Dry P-traps in unused bathrooms and floor drains let sewer gas straight into the room, and refilling the trap solves it in ten seconds. A blocked or improperly terminated plumbing vent produces the same smell more persistently. Condensate lines that have grown biological material produce a sour smell whenever the system runs. A dead rodent inside a wall cavity or duct run produces a strong odour that resolves on its own in two to four weeks and can be located and removed sooner.</p>
<p>We will check those first. It is a poor outcome for everyone if you pay for hydroxyl treatment on a house that needed a cup of water poured down a floor drain.</p>
"""

